WEBVTT

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.760
Common telemetry sources include operating systems, endpoints, applications,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.610
identity providers, network devices, cloud services, databases, infrastructure platforms,

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:10.840
and security controls.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.070
Each source offers a different view of activity and has its own strengths, gaps, formats,

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.785
and retention characteristics.

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:20.710
Identity systems can show sign-ins, authentication failures, privilege changes,

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.910
token activity, and account administration.

00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:27.225
Applications may record requests, errors, transactions, administrative actions,

00:00:27.225 --> 00:00:29.190
and access to sensitive functions.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:32.970
Network devices can describe communications between systems and locations.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.945
Security tools such as endpoint detection and response platforms, firewalls,

00:00:36.945 --> 00:00:37.790
email gateways,

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:42.348
and cloud security services often enrich raw events with classifications or alerts.

00:00:42.348 --> 00:00:44.750
A SIEM can centralize and correlate these sources,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.250
but it can only analyze the data that is actually collected and understood.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.360
Collection planning begins with security questions and important assets.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.665
Teams identify which activity must be observable, select authoritative sources,

00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:57.370
document ownership and dependencies,

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.410
and confirm that expected events arrive with enough context to support detection and

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:02.270
investigation.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.355
Maintaining a telemetry catalog makes this work repeatable.

00:01:05.355 --> 00:01:09.750
A useful catalog records source owners, covered assets, event types, important fields,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:13.610
collection paths, parsing status, retention, access restrictions, cost,

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.990
and dependent detections.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:19.090
This documentation helps engineers assess the impact of changes and helps analysts find

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.920
the best available evidence during an investigation.
